=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The helpdesk ticket claims router4 has crashed and Location C is completely isolated, but direct inspection of every component in the alleged failure path reveals a fully healthy network. Router4's container is running, its critical interfaces are up and correctly addressed: eth0 (sw4_r4 segment) carries 192.168.200.1/24, serving as the default gateway for all Location C hosts (pc11–pc14); eth4 (link_r4_r5) is up at 192.168.15.2/24, peering with router5; and eth5 (link_r4_r3) is up at 172.25.1.1/24, peering with router3. The FRR RIPv2 daemon is active and router4's routing table is fully populated with learned routes to all remote subnets (10.16.1.0/24, 10.20.1.0/24, 10.50.1.0/24, 172.16.x.x, 172.20.1.0/24, 192.168.1.0/24, 192.168.100.0/24) via both uplinks. All four Location C end hosts (pc11–pc14) ping their gateway at 192.168.200.1 with 0% packet loss, and cross-site pings from pc11 to router0 and from pc14 to router6 also succeed with 0% packet loss. The root cause is that there is no fault: the ticket describes a situation that does not exist in the live network, and the report of "router4 being down" is not supported by any observable evidence. No corrective action is required; the network is operating normally.
=== END ===